# Build output.
#
# `sh/build --to <dir>` is how binaries are meant to be produced, and it puts them
# where you ask. A bare `go build` in a module leaves one beside its source named
# after the command, and those are what these patterns catch — several megabytes
# each, rebuilt from source in seconds, and never the same twice across platforms.
/*/cmd/*/[a-z]*
!/*/cmd/*/*.go
!/*/cmd/*/*.md

# `go build` in a module root, which names the binary after the module directory.
/Orc/orc
/Anno/anno
/Dock/dock
/Mailman/mailman
/Macmuffin/muff
/Orcprobe/orcprobe
/Communique/cq

# What a session leaves behind: a curl cookie jar, and the log of a server started
# with nohup. Both are one operator's afternoon rather than part of the tree.
cookies.txt
*.log

# Go's own.
*.test
*.out
